<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enlil</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enlil was the chief god of the Sumerian pantheon, lord of the wind and air, whose temple, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekur">Ekur</a>, stood at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>. Kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> sought his recognition at Nippur to legitimise their rule over the land, and in myth he decreed fates and sent the flood. His wife was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninlil">Ninlil</a>, and his authority later passed largely to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> in Babylonia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enlil.jpg" alt="Enlil" width="320" height="517" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:God_Enlil,_seated,_from_Nippur,_Iraq._1800-1600_BCE._Iraq_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
